  "live_records": {
    "q-85407": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85407",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6})(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{2w})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{2w-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{2(w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}}{w-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
